WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:29.790 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  as I played Rainbow six siege for the  first time recently  one question crossed my mind why did it  take me two years to try this at this  point I've been hooked for weeks I  practiced as often as I can  I returned time and time again to this  complex and rewarding shooter and I'm  still finding reasons to love it it's a  marvel of modern game design upon  further investigation I discovered I was  not at all the only one since its launch  in December 2015  it has tripled its total player base  accumulating 25 million users in all 2.3  million of them play it every day for  comparison blizzards overwatch one of  the more successful multiplayer games on  the market has a little more than 30  million players  Rainbow six isn't that far behind its  community is not just alive but thriving  with memes and in jokes of its own  centered around some of the more  endearing ly useless playable characters  [Music]  honey there's nothing to fear  I think siege flew under the radar for a  while because of several reasons first  off it's worth noting that there wasn't  much height building up to its release  in fact many actively resented Rainbow  six siege because in 2011 Ubisoft showed  off Rainbow six Patriots promising a  story-driven morally complex shooter  with single-player potential when the  publisher announced the shift to a  multiplayer based experience in 2014 it  rubbed many people the wrong way seed  was fighting an uphill battle before it  even released secondly its aesthetic  doesn't do it many favors while the  Kevlar padding and riot shields are  appealing for people who prefer the  military look they don't really convey  what the game actually is a hero shooter  if you took overwatch made it ten times  harder more punishing and focused  largely on close quarters combat with  destructible environments you kind of  have Rainbow six siege lastly there's  the unfortunate story of the game's  launch server issues plagued its first  few weeks while a thin supply of maps  and characters meant it was widely  overlooked it had the skeleton of a  great game but it's bones were brittle  and almost ready to break but here's the  thing Ubisoft Montreal didn't give up it  rolled out patches and updates bug fixes  and server maintenance it promised a  year of content that would add new  characters and maps throughout the  coming months little by little it's Mia  culpa won fans back it became one of  Triple A games weirdest anomalies and  one of the greatest comeback stories in  the medium that's largely because  Ubisoft Montreal listened to fans in  numerous updates on its blog the company  outlined the roadmap for the year's  content it gave the usual patch notes  but also detailed exactly how those  updates would meet player feedback after  steadily building its player base during  2016  Ubisoft continued through 2017 as well  and as of this video has plans for the  entirety of next year - boo  [Music]  in a day and age when games are designed  to have longer lifespans offering  players continual content throughout the  years after release Rainbow six siege  puts Titans such as destiny - and the  division to absolute shame it releases  new characters and maps with one season  of content and continues to balance the  changes well into the next Ubisoft even  postponed a whole season to make way for  operation health a maintenance period  reviled by some fans but in the long  term still a sign of goodwill on  Ubisoft's part it's also worth noting  that all of this content releases for  free yes you can spend real-world money  on specific cosmetic items and to boost  the unlock rate of new characters siege  also provides an array of bonuses for  buying a $30 yearly pass but unlike  games such as Battlefront 2 or a  fortnight Rainbow six siege doesn't feel  built around this progression system at  all it is a deep intricate game with  potential for thousands of hours of  playtime at no additional cost a common  complaint with destiny or the division  another Ubisoft published title is that  the games themselves aren't all that fun  on their own they rely mainly on their  progression aspects to keep players  hooked when they don't have solid  mission and level design as a foundation  to start from people take notice they  get bored they play other games this may  sound obvious but I think a huge reason  see just thriving is because it is a  thrilling game in itself it's deep  complex unforgiving rewarding and  exhilarating over the course of one  match I can honestly say that I would  still come back to siege even if it  didn't have unlocks and progression  options I could not say the same for  destiny to the division or battlefront  to siege takes time to learn and after  150 hours I'm nowhere near seeing every  possible permutation of the map's  operators and tactical possibilities at  play with 18 offensive characters 18  defensive operators and most importantly  no respawns every little action could  mean eventual victory or sudden doom  take this match for example I'm using  Valkyrie the Navy SEAL known for her  portable cameras that the defensive team  can cycle through on their viewing  devices at first I placed the cameras on  walls and ceilings at vital choke points  as the match continues however a  teammate shows me something new  I can shred the upper part of a wall  toss one of my cameras into the gap and  now I can see two full rooms with no  sheetrock in the way I doubled my field  of view with a trick I never considered  before of course in this match it didn't  really help me because my teammates shot  me square in the face but anyway it was  a cool trick regardless here's a bigger  picture example one of my favorite  operators IQ uses a device that can scan  for electronic objects through walls  floors and ceilings it's super useful  against characters such as Jaeger and  bandit heroes with defensive gadgets  that make life harder for any attacking  team but on house a map set in a serene  suburb IQ scanning device becomes even  more useful most of the walls floors and  ceilings are made of wood and so you can  destroy defensive objects easier with  bullet penetration by gauging the  scenario selecting the right operator  and using her device to the fullest I  helped my team to a tense victory and in  a game that almost demands communication  among teammates Rainbow six siege has a  community that's oddly willing to  embrace newcomers  there are definitely elitist players who  look down on newbies but I've also seen  countless videos of people being guided  through their first few matches learning  the ins and outs of environmental  destruction and bomb defusal now here's  the best part of all this and I think  this is the secret to why Rainbow six  siege continues to grow if you started  playing siege today it would not feel  like a two-year old title the game has  been continually updated to remain  relevant it has a welcoming community  willing to teach newcomers and there are  millions of other rookies also going  through the same learning process as you  we live in a time when games are often  more concerned with being platforms than  discrete experiences destiny warframe  and overwatch all follow this ethos but  for a game to truly pull it off  despite its mediocre start is something  truly amazing to see I could go on  forever but I just want to say that I  think Rainbow six siege is the  culmination of many things I love about  modern games it has a diverse cast a  thriving community and it's built for  emergent situations like the ones I  mentioned above it's one of those games  where you keep discovering uncharted  depths of  time you play it's been a long road  since it's shaky launch but now there's  no question Rainbow six siege is here to  stay

